## Current UX Risks

### Utility Panel Risk

There is a risk the UI becomes too infrastructure-focused.

Users should feel:

- I captured a meeting and instantly received organized outcomes.

Users should NOT feel:

- I operated an AI audio processing pipeline.

Implementation details such as:

- Chunking.
- Retry logic.
- WebSocket behavior.
- Internal processing mechanics.

Should remain secondary unless troubleshooting is needed.

### Transcript Dominance Risk

Raw transcript panels are visually noisy.

Users believe they want transcripts.
What they actually value:

- Decisions.
- Tasks.
- Accountability.
- Follow-up.
- Searchable memory.

Transcript visibility should remain available but should not overpower actionable insights.

### Product Sophistication vs Visual Polish

The backend and product thinking are becoming increasingly sophisticated:

- Structured insights.
- Confidence scoring.
- Reprocessing.
- Workflow awareness.
- Future integrations.

The visual design now needs refinement to match the intelligence of the system.

Areas needing continued polish:

- Typography hierarchy.
- Spacing consistency.
- Density control.
- Status communication.
- Card hierarchy.
- Window layout behavior.
- Theme consistency.

## Live Capture UX Review

Current assessment:

- Functionality appears strong.
- Visual hierarchy needs improvement.
- Too many UI areas currently compete equally for attention.

Current UI contains simultaneously:

- Meeting configuration.
- Status.
- Timer.
- Audio levels.
- Transcript.
- Insights.
- Theme controls.
- Recording controls.

This creates excessive attention competition.

Current recommendation:

Refactor the page into clearer zones:

Top section:

- Meeting title.
- Recording state.
- Elapsed time.

Middle section:

- Transcript.
- Speaker activity.
- Realtime context.

Right rail:

- AI insights.
- Action items.
- Decisions.
- Dates.
- Risks.

Bottom sticky control area:

- Record.
- Pause.
- Add note.
- Bookmark.

The page should feel workflow-oriented rather than panel-oriented.

The user should always intuitively know:

- Current meeting state.
- What matters now.
- What the AI has already identified.

